<!-- This renderer supplies English interface labels through en-US only.
     Keep authored worksheet prose in British English. -->
<!-- FIRST DRAFT, before any cuts for time.
     Workshop 7 covers Week 8 videos 4-6 (polar form and de Moivre's theorem,
     exponential form, logarithms of complex numbers) and Week 9 videos 1 and
     3 (nth roots, hyperbolic functions). Multiple-angle formulae (Week 9
     video 2) are left for Workshop 8, alongside powers of sine and cosine
     (video 4). Week 9 video 5 also comes after this workshop.
     Notation follows the lecture slides: j, z = x + jy, the exponential form
     r e^{j theta}, the principal argument with -pi < theta <= pi, radians
     throughout, ln z with all of its values, cosh and sinh. Numbers are
     written 2 + 3j; j goes first only before a letter or a surd. The sheet
     says arctan, as Workshop 6 does.
     The slides call e^{j pi} + 1 = 0 "Euler's formula" and the formula sheet
     calls e^{j theta} = cos(theta) + j sin(theta) "Euler's relations", so
     the sheet says "exponential form" throughout.
     One chain of numbers runs through the sheet. Exercise 1's four numbers
     return in Exercises 3, 4 and 5; the roots of Exercise 2 return in
     Exercise 5(c) and in the challenge.
     Plan: about 30 minutes of core work, counting discussion parts double,
     plus an optional challenge. Each page heading names the highest Bloom
     level that page reaches; the warm-up stays at Understand and Apply. -->
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <xi:include href="docinfo.ptx"/>
  <article xml:id="worksheet07wks">
    <title>Engineering Maths 1</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>University of Bristol — EMAT10100</p>
      <p>Use the printer icon for a copy with working space. Give exact answers unless stated otherwise.</p>
    </introduction>
    <worksheet>
      <title>Workshop 7: Polar form, roots and hyperbolic functions</title>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Warm-up</title>
          <p>Please work in collaboration with your classmates to complete the following exercises. This means sharing ideas and asking each other questions.</p>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Understand, then Apply (Week 8 videos 4-5). P and Q are read
             from the Argand diagram, which is the data here; the other two
             are converted from x + jy. Q returns in Exercise 4, the other
             two in Exercise 3, and P and (d) are two of the roots found in
             Exercise 5. The "-twocol" id sets the tasks two to a row
             (assets/custom.css), so each number has its own space. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-forms-twocol">
          <introduction>
            <p>Write each number in exponential form <m>re^{j\theta}</m>, with <m>-\pi \lt \theta \le \pi</m>. The numbers <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are shown on the Argand diagram.</p>
            <image source="argand-points.svg" width="36%">
              <description>An Argand diagram on a square grid. The real axis, labelled Re, and the imaginary axis, labelled Im, are each marked from minus 3 to 3. The point P is on the imaginary axis at minus 2. The point Q is 2 units to the left of the imaginary axis and 2 units below the real axis.</description>
            </image>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p><m>P</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p><m>Q</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p><m>1+j</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p><m>-\sqrt{3}+j</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <!-- Apply (Week 8 video 3, then video 5). The roots are the two
             complex cube roots of 1. They return in Exercise 5(c) and in
             the challenge. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-review" workspace="1.4in">
          <statement>
            <p>Find the roots of <m>z^2+z+1=0</m> and write them in exponential form.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Analyse: Products and Powers</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Analyse (Week 8 video 4). The arguments in part (a)
             add up to more than pi, so the answer has to be brought back
             into range. Part (b) is de Moivre's theorem, and its answer is
             the number whose cube roots Exercise 5 finds. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-polar">
          <introduction>
            <p>This exercise uses <m>z_1=1+j</m> and <m>z_2=-\sqrt{3}+j</m> from Exercise 1.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Find <m>z_1z_2</m> in exponential form, with <m>-\pi \lt \theta \le \pi</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Find <m>z_2^3</m> in the form <m>x+jy</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.6in">
            <statement><p>Explain in your own words what happens to a point on the Argand diagram when you multiply it by <m>z_1</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Evaluate: Arguments and Logarithms</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Evaluate (Week 8 videos 4 and 6). Student A trusts
             arctan without looking at the quadrant, the trap that video 4
             warns about. Student B's rule gives an argument of z^2, but
             not the principal one. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-logs">
          <introduction>
            <p>The number <m>Q</m> in Exercise 1 is <m>z=-2-2j</m>.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>Find all the values of <m>\ln(z)</m> and circle the principal value.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.4in">
            <statement>
              <p>Two students work with the same <m>z</m>.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><p>Student A: <m>\arctan\left(\dfrac{-2}{-2}\right)=\arctan(1)=\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m>, so the principal argument of <m>z</m> is <m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Student B: the principal argument of <m>z^2</m> is twice the principal argument of <m>z</m>.</p></li>
              </ul>
              <p>Explain in your own words what each student has got wrong.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Analyse: Roots of Complex Numbers</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Analyse (Week 9 video 1). Two of the three roots
             are P and (d) from Exercise 1, and 8j is the answer to Exercise
             3(b). The diagram is blank axes for the students' own plot, not
             a figure of the answer. Part (c)'s number is a root from
             Exercise 2; part (d) prepares the challenge. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-roots">
          <introduction>
            <p>The number <m>8j</m> has three cube roots.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement><p>Find all three roots in exponential form.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
              <p>Plot the three roots on this Argand diagram.</p>
              <image source="blank-argand.svg" width="34%">
                <description>Blank Argand diagram: a horizontal real axis labelled Re and a vertical imaginary axis labelled Im crossing at the centre, with no scale marked.</description>
              </image>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p>Going anticlockwise round your diagram, each root is the previous root multiplied by the same number. What is that number?</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement><p>Add the three roots. Explain in your own words how your diagram shows the answer.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Create: Hyperbolic Functions</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply (Week 9 video 3), straight from the definitions of cosh
             and sinh. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-hyperbolic-twocol">
          <introduction>
            <p>Write each of these as a fraction.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.4in">
            <statement><p><m>\cosh(\ln(3))</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.4in">
            <statement><p><m>\sinh(\ln(3))</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <!-- Create, then Analyse. Every pair makes a different problem for
             each other. Adding the two fractions gives a back, because
             cosh(x) + sinh(x) = e^x. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-create">
          <introduction>
            <p>Work with a neighbour on this exercise. If you are working alone, find a way to get your number <m>a</m> back from your two fractions.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Choose a whole number <m>a</m> bigger than 3 and keep it to yourself. Write <m>\cosh(\ln(a))</m> and <m>\sinh(\ln(a))</m> as fractions.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>Swap fractions with a neighbour and work out their number <m>a</m>. Then swap back and check each other's answers.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Challenge: Mains Electricity</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Deep. Britain's mains supply is three-phase at 50 Hz, which
             gives the 100 pi. The number w solves z^2 + z + 1 = 0 (Exercise
             2), and each voltage is the real part of A e^{100 pi j t} times
             1, w or w^2. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-challenge">
          <introduction>
            <p>Suppose that mains electricity reaches a factory along three cables, and that the voltages on the cables at time <m>t</m> seconds are
            <md>
              <mrow>V_1 \amp= A\cos(100\pi t)</mrow>
              <mrow>V_2 \amp= A\cos\left(100\pi t+\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)</mrow>
              <mrow>V_3 \amp= A\cos\left(100\pi t+\frac{4\pi}{3}\right)</mrow>
            </md>
            for some constant <m>A</m>. Each voltage is a third of a cycle ahead of the one before.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Let <m>w=e^{2\pi j/3}</m>, which is one of the roots from Exercise 2. Show that <m>1+w+w^2=0</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.4in">
            <statement><p>Each voltage is the real part of a complex number. Use part (a) to show that <m>V_1+V_2+V_3=0</m> at every time <m>t</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <conclusion>
            <p>When the three cables feed equal loads, the three currents cancel in the same way. This is why the cables on a pylon come in threes, with no return cable.</p>
          </conclusion>
        </exercise>
      </page>
    </worksheet>
  </article>
</pretext>
